id: decisions/018-who-may-authorise
title: "A bot may own the work before the say-so, never the say-so"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'who-may-authorise'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: who-may-authorise
axis_gloss: "whether an effect needs a named human principal's say-so, or an agent/automation may license its own action"
poles:
  a: principal-authority
  b: agent-autonomy
split: lone-exception
draft_status: full
leverage: 13.9
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(29) x split_weight(0.4) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.2)"

question: >
  May any bot or automation license an effect on its own, or may it only own the
  proposing, routing and evidence-gathering that comes before a principal's
  authorization — and does a bot-owned open question count as owning an effect?

why_it_matters: >
  Every effect that matters — commit, publish, spend, canonicalize, ratify — is
  held behind a named human's current exact authorization in the written rule,
  yet two artifacts put a bot in the owner seat (a pipeline that proposes before
  review, an open question owned by YAWN.bot) without saying whether owning is
  authorizing. Once answered, every child Yawn and steward bot below Dave knows
  exactly which seat it may sit in: it may own a question, a proposal or a
  pipeline stage, and the answer or the effect still waits for a principal.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    27 judgments sit at principal-authority (22 authored) and 2 at agent-autonomy
    (0 authored). The repository asserts both directions on this axis.
  attributed_to: TAXONOMY.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether Dave wants bots to be nameable as `owner:` of anything at all, or only
    as `assigned_to:`; and whether the pre-review pipeline stages (extraction,
    retrieval, structural proposal) are effects when they write files to the tree.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      rule: Only a named human principal may authorize an effect. A bot or
      automation may read, orient, mirror, infer, ask, classify, route and propose;
      it may not be named `owner:` of an open question or of any pipeline stage that
      produces an authorized event. Bot-owned questions are reassigned to a
      principal and the bot is listed as `assigned_to:` for evidence gathering.
    cost: "Reassign the projection-equivalence question in questions/open-questions.yawn from YAWN.bot to Dave; add assigned_to to templates/open-question.yawn; every open question in a child Yawn needs a human owner even when only the runtime can gather its evidence."
    risk: "A holarchy of steward bots cannot hold an open question about its own runtime, so runtime questions go unowned or get a nominal human owner who cannot answer them."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "tests/effect-authority.test.mjs: every `owner:` under open_questions in questions/*.yawn resolves to a human principal; every automation/*.yawn allowed_effects entry that names commit, push, merge, publish, send, spend or mutate carries an authorization_ref; the existing 'AI-authored moves remain proposals until a separate authorization receipt exists' (tests/agency-holarchy-v0.2.test.mjs) and 'only authorized events update canonical materialized state' (tests/state-substrate-v1.test.mjs) stay green."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0005"
      - "J0014"
      - "J0022"
      - "J0030"
      - "J0113"
      - "J0139"
  - name: B
    description: >
      rule: An automation may license its own effect inside a declared envelope
      without a per-act authorization (commit to its branch, send its notification,
      answer the question it owns), as the import pipeline and the bot-owned open
      question already do. Each automation declares `autonomous_envelope:`; effects
      inside it need no receipt.
    cost: "Strike or narrow blocked_without_current_exact_authorization in agents/yawn.bot.yawn; rewrite the commit_gate in automation/yawn.bot.feedback-loop.yawn; the execution-relationship schema's selfAuthorizationAllowed const false must be relaxed and its test rewritten."
    risk: "The safety rail 'ai output is not authority' becomes conditional on an envelope a bot could widen by editing its own record; the interception governance test 'approval is one-time conditional preauthorization, never recurring authority' would have to be reversed."
    reversibility: false
    proof_needed: "A test that every automation declares autonomous_envelope and every ledger entry's effect falls inside it; reversal of tests/interception-statement-image-governance.test.mjs 'the recurring contract can propose but cannot generate, spend, or publish' and of the selfAuthorizationAllowed const in schemas/execution-relationship.v1.schema.json."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0397"
      - "J0545"
  - name: C
    description: >
      rule: Only a named human principal may authorize an effect (as A). carve-out,
      written down: a bot or automation may be the `owner:` of an open question or
      of a pre-authorization pipeline stage, and ownership means answerable for
      gathering evidence and proposing — never authority over the answer or the
      effect. templates/open-question.yawn gains `authority: none` and
      `accepted_by:` beside `owner:`; examples/conversation-import-routing.yawn
      states that steps before human_review produce proposals only;
      agents/yawn.bot.yawn may_without_external_authority gains 'own an open
      question's evidence gathering'.
    cost: "Three small edits (open-question template, the import example, agents/yawn.bot.yawn) and one added field on the four existing open questions."
    risk: "A reader skims `owner: YAWN.bot` and reads it as authority anyway; the added `authority: none` line is the only defence."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "tests/effect-authority.test.mjs: every open question with a non-human owner carries authority: none and accepted_by naming a principal; every pipeline step before human_review in examples/conversation-import-routing.yawn carries the word proposal in its rule; no automation allowed_effects entry names an effect from agents/yawn.bot.yawn blocked_without_current_exact_authorization without an authorization_ref."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0022"
      - "J0030"
      - "J0113"
      - "J0397"
      - "J0545"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.75
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    Side A holds 27 judgments, 22 authored, and the passing suite enforces it six
    ways (state-substrate 'only authorized events update canonical state',
    agency-holarchy 'AI-authored moves remain proposals', objective-holon
    'confidence cannot substitute for an accepted ratification receipt',
    delegated-execution 'receipt graduation proposes but never activates policy',
    interception 'approval is one-time', collaboration-message 'keeps model
    authority closed'), plus selfAuthorizationAllowed const false in the
    execution-relationship schema. Side B is two REVEALED readings and neither
    licenses an effect: the import pipeline places human_review before
    authorized_events, and the bot-owned question gathers evidence for a receipt
    Dave will accept. So the artifacts do not contradict the rule; they leave one
    word — owner — undefined. C keeps the rule untouched and writes the one
    exception where it lives. A is nearly as good and differs only in whether a
    bot may be named owner of a question; in a holarchy of steward bots that is
    the seat they need. B is ruled out by the constitution's own rails.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "questions/open-questions.yawn: the entry owned by YAWN.bot carries authority: none and accepted_by: Dave, and templates/open-question.yawn teaches both fields."
    - "examples/conversation-import-routing.yawn: every step before human_review says its output is a proposal."
    - "A test in tests/ rejects an open question with a non-human owner and no authority: none, rejects an automation allowed_effects entry naming a blocked effect without an authorization_ref, and passes."

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    A ledger under records/ shows a commit, publication, send or spend by an
    automation with no authorization_ref, or an open question owned by a bot is
    marked answered-with-evidence on the bot's own say-so, and npm test stays
    green.
  status: proposed

evidence_sample:
  - "J0005"
  - "J0014"
  - "J0022"
  - "J0030"
  - "J0113"
  - "J0139"
  - "J0397"
  - "J0545"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
  id_index: TAXONOMY.md
